  "work": "To find the value of $\\sin \\left( 2\\tan^{-1} (x) \\right)$, we use the double-angle identity for sine: $\\sin(2\\theta) = 2\\sin(\\theta)\\cos(\\theta)$. Let $\\theta = \\tan^{-1}(x)$. Then, $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1 + x^2}}$ and $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + x^2}}$. Substituting these into the identity, we get $\\sin(2\\tan^{-1}(x)) = 2 \\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1 + x^2}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + x^2}} = \\frac{2x}{1 + x^2}$.",
  "draft": "The value of $\\sin \\left( 2\\tan^{-1} (x) \\right)$ is $\\frac{2x}{1 + x^2}$. This result is derived using the double-angle identity for sine, $\\sin(2\\theta) = 2\\sin(\\theta)\\cos(\\theta)$, and substituting the trigonometric values of $\\tan^{-1}(x)$, which are $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1 + x^2}}$ and $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + x^2}}$."
}

